Linux retains DIR_APP_DICTIONARIES as the writable user-data location. Open and
validate that dictionary first, deleting corrupt writable copies as before.
If unavailable, open DIR_ASSETS/Dictionaries/<versioned-language>.bdic read-only
and run the same BDict::Verify validation. Never delete an invalid packaged
copy. Return the writable location on failure so the existing initialization,
download URL, download, and retry behavior remain unchanged. No switch disables
spellchecking or language selection. Windows, Android and platform spellcheck
behavior remain unchanged.

The package MUST include real en-US-10-2.bdic bytes next to the Linux executable
under Dictionaries/. The required install step, exact source revision, SHA-256
inputs and redistribution notices are in ci/chromium-dictionaries.toml. That
config is a packaging contract with an executable install_command, not an
implicitly consumed GN setting. Run it after staging chrome and before creating
the final browser archive. Without that step the normal download still occurs.

Dictionary input observed locally:
  repository https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/deps/hunspell_dictionaries.git
  revision cee14e319bb7603a1157bb4d1e216be64ee82b77 (pinned by DEPS:2430-2431)
  path en-US-10-2.bdic
  bytes 451982
  sha256 b0d80e6b6c5491879365e176ecbd98074602a663429bcfcfb0a5a8375c051f13
  source URL https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/deps/hunspell_dictionaries/+/cee14e319bb7603a1157bb4d1e216be64ee82b77/en-US-10-2.bdic

License evidence: README_en_US.txt:88-109,173-205,288-344 supplies SCOWL,
WordNet, VarCon and modified-Ispell BSD notices and distribution conditions.
README.chromium:49-59 documents BDICT derivation; :89-118 records en_US delta
additions under the existing COPYING GPL/LGPL/MPL tri-license. Ship complete
README_en_US.txt, README.chromium, LICENSE and COPYING, not only the generic
README license label. The package command also preserves en_US.aff, en_US.dic
and en_US.dic_delta and marks the Chromium-derived binary as modified in NOTICE.
No dictionary regeneration or fake fixture is used.

Source trace (relative to source base):
